# The Hammond B3 as a stop for dacvoice (../voices/README.md, "Stops" and
# "Recorded instruments"; ../DESIGN.md section 27): the tonewheels are sines,
# the drawbars the ranks of a 16-foot frame (16' rank 1, 5 1/3' 3, 8' 2,
# 4' 4, 2 2/3' 6, 2' 8, 1 3/5' 10, 1 1/3' 12, 1' 16), each drawbar step 3
# dB.
# Pulled out 88 8000 000, the full bottom three is a plausible starting
# point for A Whiter Shade of Pale, not a documented registration.
# The Leslie at its slow speed (chorale), about 0.8 Hz.
# Note that is a copyrighted song, not included here.
# You can find midi files online and create a version for your own use.
# Don't distribute it, that is a violation of copyright law.
name B3 slow
family Leslie slow
n 10
frame 16
start 40 dB
attack 0.005 s
peak 0 dB
decay 0 dB/s
sustain 0 dB
release 0.02 s
# The key click: a few ms of noise across the upper harmonics.
chiff 0.003 s 14 dB 8 64
harmonics 1 1.0
rank 1 1.0
rank 3 1.0
rank 2 1.0
level 0 dB
# The Leslie: the slow counter free running at the rotor's speed, a little
# pitch swing (the horn's Doppler), the level and the place swinging.
vibrato 0.8 Hz 4 cents free
leslie 2 dB 4 dB
# No interpolate: interpolated, its four-note chords take 80% of the
# channel and the last converter's samples come late (owner, 11-Oct-2026).
